00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:18.905
to figure out who believes what,

00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:20.406
and why they believe it?

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:22.876
- I do like to cite authors
that actually do the work,

00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:24.077
unlike some authors,

00:02:24.077 --> 00:02:25.011
(Dave chuckling)

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:25.879
whom I won't name. (chuckling)

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:26.946
Not you.

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:27.780
(chuckling) I mean, just...

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:28.882
There's some academics

00:02:28.882 --> 00:02:30.383 align:start size:88% position:13%
that's not...
- Thank you, thank you.

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:31.651
- There's some academics that
are not especially generous

00:02:31.651 --> 00:02:33.753
about recognizing where
they got that research.

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:36.222 align:start size:88% position:13%
There's actually a
huge body of literature

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:38.224
on conspiracies and
conspiracy theories,

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:40.393 align:start size:84% position:16%
why people believe
them, sociology of it,

00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:42.862 align:start size:88% position:13%
social psych, cognitive
psych, you know?

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:44.430 align:start size:84% position:16%
Anthropology of
beliefs, and so on,

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.665
there's a lot in there.

00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:47.100
So, I summarize all that,

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.801
but then try to push the
frontiers, a little bit,

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:50.370
with some of my own ideas.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.173 align:start size:97% position:3%
I constructed a three-part,
tripartite, you might call it,

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:56.409 align:start size:84% position:16%
or three-legged-stool
of conspiricism,

00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:59.045
proxy conspiricism,
tribal conspiricism

00:02:59.045 --> 00:03:00.914
and constructive conspiricism.

00:03:00.914 --> 00:03:04.150 align:start size:97% position:3%
So, there's, kind of, an
overarching theoretical model,

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:05.852
that holds together all
the different parts.

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:08.087
So, while most of the
details, of course,

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:09.956
are other people's research,

00:03:09.956 --> 00:03:11.224
I've kind of put
it all together,

00:03:11.224 --> 00:03:14.460 align:start size:88% position:13%
in an integrative whole,
under that rubric.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.995
- Yeah, and I really
liked how you did that.

00:03:15.995 --> 00:03:18.598
So, as you said there're sort
of three sections to the book,

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:20.233
a prologue that leads into it,

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:23.036 align:start size:97% position:3%
but then, each one, sort
of, leads into the next one.

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:26.306
So, I wanna get into some of
that, but broadly speaking,

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:28.741
one of the questions I
get the most, these days,

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:29.976
if I do a public event,

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:32.312
or if I'm doing a Q&amp;A
online, or whatever,

00:03:32.312 --> 00:03:34.514 align:start size:97% position:3%
is, people say to me:
"Where do you get your news?

00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:36.749
How do you know
what to believe?"

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:38.218
And, I find,

00:03:38.218 --> 00:03:41.054 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's getting increasingly
more difficult to answer that.

00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:42.488
I, you know,

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:44.557 align:start position:0%
we're all going into our little
ideological echo-chambers,

00:03:44.557 --> 00:03:47.126
I do my best to not be
in there all the time,

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:49.128 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it's gettin'
tough for everybody,

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:52.532
and as I often say, if
you're watching MSNBC,

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:54.067
you, basically,

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.736
live in a different world
as the Fox News person.

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:59.305 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think everyone, right
now, in a modern sense,

00:03:59.305 --> 00:04:00.773
is struggling with this.

00:04:00.773 --> 00:04:03.109 align:start size:97% position:3%
So, the book, for that
reason, feels very relevant,

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:05.278
'cause I think everyone's
going through some version

00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:08.381 align:start size:88% position:13%
of "a conspiracy
theory", to some extent.

00:04:08.381 --> 00:04:10.650 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, well, so
here's one solution.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.052 align:start size:84% position:16%
I actually get
newspapers, every day.

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:14.254
I get the New York Times,

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:15.888
or, see, The New York...

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:17.690 align:start size:84% position:16%
Oh, where's my Wall
Street Journal?

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:18.891
Here's my Wall Street...

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:19.892 align:start size:84% position:16%
Okay, I got my Wall
Street Journal.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:21.127
Oh, here it is,

00:04:21.127 --> 00:04:22.629
and the Wall Street
Journal, there we go.

00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:24.764
So, I add 'em up and divide
by two, to get the truth.

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:26.633
(both laughing)

00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:28.001
- So, in my estimation,

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:29.502
the Wall Street Journal

00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:31.504
would be far more factual
than the New York Times,

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:33.640
which, I think, has gone
really off the deep end,

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:34.540
as you know.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:35.675
You know, Bari Weiss,

00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:36.776
they were so outta control,

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:38.011
that Bari Weiss, as a liberal,

00:04:38.011 --> 00:04:40.079 align:start size:84% position:16%
felt she couldn't even
stay there anymore.

00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:41.314
- Yeah.

00:04:41.314 --> 00:04:42.949 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So, when you pick
up the New York Times,

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:44.984 align:start size:84% position:16%
are you basically
going, "Alright, they
screwed this one up.

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:48.788
Ah, that's a deflection,
this is a confusion", etc?

00:04:48.788 --> 00:04:50.490 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well, the Wall Street
Journal, you know,

00:04:50.490 --> 00:04:54.694 align:start size:97% position:3%
separating the opinion
section from the news section,

00:04:54.694 --> 00:04:56.429 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know they're both
biased, in each direction.

00:04:56.429 --> 00:04:57.630
That we all know about,

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.098
and that's true

00:04:59.098 --> 00:05:00.667 align:start size:94% position:6%
for any other news sources
you might wanna name.

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:01.801
You have to separate the...

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:03.136
Like, just even Fox News,

00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:06.539
if you just take the
daytime news reporters,

00:05:06.539 --> 00:05:08.541 align:start size:84% position:16%
they're very different
from the evening.

00:05:08.541 --> 00:05:10.043
More like entertainers.

00:05:10.043 --> 00:05:11.511
So, I think if you know
what you're looking at...

00:05:11.511 --> 00:05:14.447 align:start size:97% position:3%
But, my point is, you can't
rely on any one source.

00:05:14.447 --> 00:05:15.982
You have to go to
multiple sources,

00:05:15.982 --> 00:05:19.018
including online sources,
and just evaluate them.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:21.954 align:start size:88% position:13%
And there are sites
that evaluate the bias,

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:25.458
liberal or conservative bias
of different media sources.

00:05:25.458 --> 00:05:27.393
You just have to
do that, you know?

00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:29.329 align:start size:84% position:16%
And NPR I don't trust
so much, anymore.

00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:33.232 align:start size:94% position:6%
AP, Associated Press, I
feel, is fairly objective,

00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:34.767
about how they present things.

00:05:34.767 --> 00:05:37.303 align:start size:97% position:3%
No one's completely
unbiased, of course, but...

00:05:37.303 --> 00:05:39.105
So, multiple sources, I mean,

00:05:39.105 --> 00:05:41.407
now there's so many, online.

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:42.575
Really, you just
have to, kind of,

00:05:42.575 --> 00:05:45.011 align:start size:88% position:13%
do a little bit of
your own fact-checking.

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:46.312 align:start size:88% position:13%
- And if you could do...
- Yeah.

00:05:46.312 --> 00:05:47.246
- Yeah.

00:05:47.246 --> 00:05:48.514
- Well, isn't that, almost,

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:50.416
one of the biggest
problems we have,

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:53.019 align:start size:94% position:6%
in this technological
adolescence that we're in,

00:05:53.019 --> 00:05:54.520
that the average person,

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:57.290
that, even if they wanna stay
informed on current events,

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:58.558
what's goin' on in the world,

00:05:58.558 --> 00:06:00.360 align:start size:84% position:16%
the economy,
whatever it might be,

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:01.594
they don't have the time.

00:06:01.594 --> 00:06:02.528
- Right.
- To go:

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:04.097
"Okay, I'm gonna read The Times,

00:06:04.097 --> 00:06:05.631
and the Wall Street Journal,

00:06:05.631 --> 00:06:07.800
and I'll see what they're
sayin' on Fox and MSNBC",

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.469
and then they just kind
of put their hands up,

00:06:09.469 --> 00:06:11.037
and they check out.

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:13.072
I think we're getting a
lot of that, actually.

00:06:13.072 --> 00:06:14.440
- So , the subtitle of the book,

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:16.476 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Why the Rational
Believe the Irrational",

00:06:16.476 --> 00:06:19.512
my point is that people that
believe conspiracy theories

00:06:19.512 --> 00:06:21.948
are not tinfoil-hat idiots,

00:06:21.948 --> 00:06:23.883
they're me and you, everybody.

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:25.318
Surveys show

00:06:25.318 --> 00:06:26.853 align:start size:97% position:3%
everybody believes at least
one conspiracy theory,

00:06:26.853 --> 00:06:28.221
and one reason for that, is,

00:06:28.221 --> 00:06:30.556 align:start position:0%
by constructive conspiracicism,
a lot of 'em are true.

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:31.391
(chuckling)

00:06:31.391 --> 00:06:32.225
So...

00:06:32.225 --> 00:06:33.493
- [Dave] Right.

00:06:33.493 --> 00:06:35.194
- It pays to be a little
constructively paranoid,

00:06:35.194 --> 00:06:37.263
just in case, but...

00:06:37.263 --> 00:06:39.198
So, for most, for the most part,

00:06:39.198 --> 00:06:42.435
most people are rational
about their daily lives.

00:06:42.435 --> 00:06:44.337 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, they get the
kids off to school,

00:06:44.337 --> 00:06:45.838
they hold jobs, you know,

00:06:45.838 --> 00:06:47.974
they keep gas in the tank
and food in the fridge,

00:06:47.974 --> 00:06:50.009
and they have a social life,

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:52.078
and they're just normal,

00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:54.113
about things that they
could do something about.

00:06:54.113 --> 00:06:56.783 align:start size:97% position:3%
Now, if you take some of
the big conspiracy theories,

00:06:56.783 --> 00:06:58.985 align:start size:84% position:16%
like the rigged
election, say of 2020.

00:07:00.186 --> 00:07:02.655
How would I check that,
to know what's going on?

00:07:02.655 --> 00:07:06.359 align:start size:97% position:3%
You have to rely on sources
that have the resources

00:07:06.359 --> 00:07:09.629 align:start size:97% position:3%
and access to check,
fact-check things like that.

00:07:09.629 --> 00:07:11.097
I wouldn't even
know who to call,

00:07:11.097 --> 00:07:13.499
in Ohio, or Georgia or
Arizona to ask, you know,

00:07:13.499 --> 00:07:16.235 align:start position:0%
"What about that truck that
pulled up at 3 in the morning?"

00:07:16.235 --> 00:07:19.138
'Cause that grainy video was
kind of suspicious, you know?

00:07:19.138 --> 00:07:21.641 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, here we rely on people
that we should trust,

00:07:21.641 --> 00:07:25.111
and we have, traditionally,
maybe not so much anymore.

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:26.479
But, for me,

00:07:26.479 --> 00:07:28.181
whatever questions I
had were put to rest

00:07:28.181 --> 00:07:30.416 align:start size:84% position:16%
when Attorney General
Bill Barr said:

00:07:30.416 --> 00:07:32.351
"Well, you know, we had
the entire resources

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:34.353 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the Department of Justice,
and we looked into it,

00:07:34.353 --> 00:07:36.322
and we looked here,
and we checked that,

00:07:36.322 --> 00:07:37.990 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so on and so forth,
and didn't find anything."

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:39.225
It's like, "Okay, well..."

00:07:39.225 --> 00:07:41.360
End of story", as far
as I was concerned.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:42.929 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now, it's not about...
- What do you say

00:07:42.929 --> 00:07:44.130
to the people that will go,

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:47.099 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Well, Bill Barr is
just part of the machine

00:07:47.099 --> 00:07:48.634
that would produce
that sort of lie."

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:50.536 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because that, of
course, as you know,

00:07:50.536 --> 00:07:51.537 align:start size:84% position:16%
you're the expert
in this, (chuckling)

00:07:51.537 --> 00:07:52.772
I mean, conspiracies,

00:07:52.772 --> 00:07:55.441 align:start size:88% position:13%
they jump from one, sort
of, seeming dead-end,

00:07:55.441 --> 00:07:57.310
to a new place.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:58.711
And then a new place, and a
new place and a new place.

